# KNOWN GAP — HIGH severity: qualified match-arm patterns
# `case Result.Ok(v):` / `case Result.Error(e):` on a `Result[T, E]` scrutinee
# generate C that reads `.Ok_0` unconditionally without checking the
# discriminant tag. Every arm falls to the Ok branch regardless of the actual
# variant → silent WRONG OUTPUT on both backends. Discovered 2026-08-06 by
# the Round XXXIII D26 F1 executor while writing capture fixtures (the D26
# fixtures use the UNQUALIFIED `case Ok(v):` form as workaround).
#
# INTENDED: the match reads the discriminant, dispatches to the correct arm,
# and prints `-42` (the Error arm hit because `pick(-1)` returns `Error`).
#
# CURRENT: both backends (C AND `--backend=llvm`) print `0` — the compiled
# code reads `Ok_0` unconditionally as an int64, returning the zero-init
# default (or garbage bytes from the Error payload). Silent type confusion
# at the match lowering; no diagnostic at any stage.
#
# DISCRIMINATOR: the QUALIFIED form (`Result.Ok(v)` / `Result.Error(e)`)
# breaks; the UNQUALIFIED form (`Ok(v)` / `Error(e)`) works correctly.
# Rewriting this fixture's arms to the unqualified form yields the intended
# `-42` output. That is the D26 fixtures' workaround pattern.
#
# SIBLING CLASS suspected: `Option.Some(v)` / `Option.None()` in qualified
# form may have the same bug (not measured; would need a separate probe).
#
# FIX SHAPE: unknown — the qualified pattern path likely bypasses the tag-
# discriminate code emit at match lowering; the unqualified path routes
# through the correct discriminator. Own scout to find the divergence in
# `src/ir/lowering/stmts/patterns.rs` (or wherever match arms lower). Fix
# probably a one-line missing tag-check in the qualified-form branch.
#
# Filed at TODO.md "Semantics / reference-grade rejection > High" as
# "Qualified match arm patterns... generate C that reads .Ok_0
# unconditionally". Un-ignore `qualified_result_match_arm_wrong_output`
# in `tests/integration.rs` when the fix lands.
Result[int, String] pick(int x):
    if x > 0:
        return Ok(x)
    return Error("bad")

void main():
    Result[int, String] r = pick(-1)
    match r:
        case Result.Ok(v): print(v)
        case Result.Error(e): print(-42)
